### 1. Introduction
The bench press is a full-body movement. Establishing a rock-solid foundation with your upper back and legs is essential for handling maximum loads without shoulder impingement.

### 2. Upper Back & Scapular Setup
- **Retract and Depress:** Pinch your shoulder blades together and pull them down into your back pockets. This creates a stable surface to press from and protects the rotator cuff.
- **Thoracic Arch:** Arch through your mid-to-upper back (thoracic spine), keeping glutes firmly on the bench at all times.

### 3. Bar Path Physics
- The optimal barbell trajectory is not a straight vertical line. It descends toward the lower sternum and follows an angled J-curve back toward the shoulders/eyes on the ascent to minimize moment arms.

### 4. Leg Drive Execution
- Plant feet firmly under or slightly behind knees.
- Cue driving your heels into the floor and pushing your body toward the head of the bench without lifting your glutes off the pad.

### 5. Common Bench Press Mistakes
- Flaring elbows to 90 degrees (keep tuck between 45 and 70 degrees).
- Bouncing the bar off the rib cage.
- Losing upper back tightness at the top of lockout.

