### Introduction
The single most damaging myth in all of physique culture is the notion that when you enter a fat loss phase, you should switch from heavy compound lifting to "high reps with light weights to burn fat and tone up." This myth has destroyed more hard-earned muscle than any other bad advice in history. Your body is a ruthless economizer. Muscle tissue is metabolically expensive—it burns roughly 13 kcal per kilogram per day just sitting on your frame, whereas fat tissue burns only 4.5 kcal/kg. If you remove the heavy mechanical tension that built your muscle in the first place, your body will happily cannibalize that muscle to conserve energy in a deficit.

### Learning Objectives
- Understand the evolutionary imperative for muscle preservation during caloric restriction.
- Recognize why mechanical tension (not metabolic fatigue) is the primary anti-catabolic signal.
- Maintain baseline working weights on core compound lifts throughout your cut.

### Detailed Explanation
In a caloric deficit, your body is in a state of net negative energy balance. If energy intake is low and you only ask your muscles to lift light 2 kg pink dumbbells for 30 reps, your brain concludes: *"We do not need large, expensive, high-threshold motor units to lift light loads. Let us dismantle these heavy muscle fibers and turn their amino acids into liver glucose."*

Conversely, when you demand that your muscles continue to lift heavy loads (e.g., 100 kg Bench Press or 140 kg RDL for 6 to 10 reps close to failure), your brain concludes: *"If we break down this muscle tissue, we will be crushed by external loads. We MUST preserve every single myofibril to survive, and burn adipose tissue instead."*

