Groundbreaking research by Dr. James Levine at the Mayo Clinic demonstrated that between two individuals of identical height and weight, NEAT can vary by up to **800 to 1,000 calories per day**. Levine discovered that when lean individuals were overfed, those who naturally resisted fat gain unconsciously increased their NEAT (spontaneous movement and pacing), incinerating the excess calories. Conversely, in a deficit, individuals who fail to track steps unconsciously drop their NEAT by 300 to 500 calories, erasing their diet deficit entirely.

### Practical Examples
