### Introduction
One of the most heated debates in the fitness community revolves around split selection. Should you train Push/Pull/Legs, Upper/Lower, or Full Body? Science reveals that the specific name of your split matters far less than how it organizes your **weekly volume, frequency, and systemic fatigue**. A well-engineered training split is an architectural blueprint designed to deliver optimal volume per muscle group while preventing overlapping fatigue from destroying your joints and central nervous system.

### Learning Objectives
- Evaluate the physiological advantages and trade-offs of Upper/Lower, Push/Pull/Legs, and Full Body splits.
- Prevent negative muscular overlap between synergist muscle groups across training days.
- Match training split structure to individual weekly schedule availability and recovery profile.
- Distribute weekly sets across sessions to maintain high set quality without crossing into junk volume.

### Detailed Explanation
A training split distributes your weekly target volume across the calendar. To evaluate any split, consider three critical criteria:
1. **Frequency per Muscle**: Does it stimulate each muscle group at least 2 times per week to match the 24-36 hour Muscle Protein Synthesis window?
2. **Session Volume Efficiency**: Does it keep volume per muscle group between 4 and 8 sets per workout, avoiding the junk volume zone (> 8-10 sets)?
3. **Fatigue Overlap Management**: Does training one muscle today interfere with another muscle tomorrow (e.g., tricep fatigue from chest pressing ruining shoulder pressing the next day)?

Here is how the three premier evidence-based splits compare:
- **The 4-Day Upper/Lower Split**: Monday (Upper A), Tuesday (Lower A), Wednesday (Rest), Thursday (Upper B), Friday (Lower B), Saturday/Sunday (Rest). Provides 72 hours of recovery between upper-body sessions and 72 hours between lower-body sessions. Low systemic fatigue; ideal for working professionals and busy lifters.
- **The 5-to-6-Day Push/Pull/Legs (PPL) Split**: Push (Chest/Shoulders/Triceps), Pull (Back/Traps/Biceps), Legs (Quads/Hamstrings/Calves). Shorter, focused workouts, but a 6-day PPL creates massive cumulative systemic and connective tissue fatigue unless run as a rotating 5-day cycle.
- **The 3-to-4-Day Full Body Split**: Spikes MPS 3 to 4 times per week; every set is executed under minimal local fatigue, but warm-up time is extensive and systemic fatigue per session is high.

### Scientific Background
A comprehensive meta-analysis by Schoenfeld et al. (2016) comparing training frequencies confirmed that **training a muscle group 2 times per week produces superior hypertrophy compared to 1 time per week**, even when total weekly set volume is equated. 

