### Introduction
Ask the average gym novice about their genetics, and you will hear phrases like: "I am an ectomorph, so I cannot build muscle" or "I am an endomorph, so my metabolism is broken." The concept of fixed somatotypes—invented in the 1940s by psychologist William Sheldon—was originally created to correlate body shape with personality traits and criminal propensity, not metabolic capacity.

Treating these categories as permanent genetic destinies is scientifically invalid. Your current body shape is a dynamic reflection of your past caloric intake, physical activity history, hormonal environment, and skeletal architecture. In this lesson, we replace unscientific archetypes with precise anthropometric baselines.

### Learning Objectives
- Deconstruct the physiological myths surrounding somatotypes and understand phenotypic plasticity.
- Measure your bony frame dimensions (wrist and ankle circumferences) to estimate natural muscular potential.
- Execute standardized circumference measurements across 5 anatomical anchor points.
