### Detailed Explanation
Human skeletal muscle consists of approximately 70–75% water, 20% protein, and 5–10% minerals, glycogen, and intramuscular lipids. When we talk about building muscle, we are referring to the synthesis of new contractile proteins (actin and myosin filaments) within existing muscle cells.

According to research and predictive models developed by exercise physiologists Lyle McDonald and Alan Aragon, the rate of natural muscle protein accretion diminishes as a lifter approaches their genetic potential:

| Training Experience | Potential Muscle Gain (Male) | Potential Muscle Gain (Female) |
| :--- | :--- | :--- |
| **Year 1 (Novice)** | 0.7 – 1.0 kg / month (~8–10 kg / year) | 0.35 – 0.5 kg / month (~4–5 kg / year) |
