| **Year 3 (Advanced)** | 0.2 – 0.25 kg / month (~2–3 kg / year) | 0.1 – 0.12 kg / month (~1–1.5 kg / year) |
| **Year 4+ (Elite)** | 0.5 – 1.0 kg / year (Approaching limit) | 0.25 – 0.5 kg / year (Approaching limit) |

*Important distinction*: When a beginner starts lifting and consuming sufficient carbohydrates and creatine, muscle cells draw in significant intracellular water and glycogen. A beginner might gain 2.5 kg on the scale in their very first month; however, approximately 1.0 kg is actual contractile tissue, while 1.5 kg represents intracellular glycogen, creatine stores, and water. This is healthy lean tissue, but it should not be confused with pure dry protein accretion.

### Scientific Background
Why is human muscle accretion limited to these rates? The rate-limiting step is ribosomal biogenesis and translation. Synthesizing new peptide chains requires transcription of DNA to mRNA, followed by ribosomal translation into amino acid sequences, folding into functional tertiary structures, and incorporation into the sarcomere. 

Furthermore, human muscle growth is regulated by genetic brake mechanisms, predominantly the myostatin gene. Myostatin is an autocrine cytokine that inhibits muscle cell growth and differentiation. Resistance training downregulates myostatin, but only within evolutionary bounds designed to prevent excessive metabolic overhead (muscle tissue is energetically expensive to maintain, consuming ~13 kcal per kg per day at rest, compared to ~4.5 kcal for adipose tissue).

