### Introduction
Once your daily caloric target and protein baseline are established, you face the next critical nutritional decision: how should the remaining calories be divided between carbohydrates and dietary fats? 

In contemporary fitness culture, dogmatic diet wars rage constantly: keto advocates claim carbohydrates make you fat, while low-fat zealots claim dietary fat clogs your arteries. For athletes and individuals seeking to transform from skinny-fat to muscular, both extremes are counterproductive. Understanding the distinct physiological roles of each macronutrient allows you to construct a balanced, whole-food eating system that fuels explosive gym performance, optimizes hormonal health, and makes meal planning effortless.

### Learning Objectives
- Allocate dietary fat intake within the healthy physiological range (0.8 to 1.2 g/kg).
- Calculate carbohydrate requirements to fully saturate skeletal muscle glycogen stores.
- Master the "Plate Architecture Model" to build balanced whole-food meals without weighing every single vegetable.
