### Scientific Background
Why is relative strength so intimately coupled with muscle size? According to the sliding filament theory and muscle biomechanics, maximal isometric and dynamic force is determined by the **Physiological Cross-Sectional Area (PCSA)** of the muscle fibers, which represents the total number of sarcomeres arranged in parallel. 

While early strength gains in months 1 and 2 are driven by neural recruitment and rate coding, once neural efficiency is established, a muscle fiber cannot generate significantly more force without synthesizing new myofibrils (actin and myosin cross-bridges). Therefore, advancing from a 16 kg dumbbell bench press to a 28 kg dumbbell bench press mathematically mandates the physical accretion of new contractile muscle tissue across the pectorals, anterior deltoids, and triceps.

### Practical Examples
**The Transformation of Julian (70 kg)**
